      "questionNumber": 16,
      "stem": "Two patients are evaluated for acute hematuria in the outpatient clinic. Patient A developed gross hematuria simultaneously with acute tonsillitis, and serum C3 was normal. Patient B developed acute nephritic syndrome, hypertension, periorbital edema, and gross hematuria 2.5 weeks after recovery from Group A Streptococcal pharyngitis, with serum C3 markedly reduced at 22 mg/dL. Which of the following statements regarding the expected clinical course and complement kinetics of Patient B compared to Patient A is CORRECT?",
      "options": [
        "A. Patient B has IgA Nephropathy; serum C3 levels will remain low permanently without recovery.",
        "B. Patient B has Post-Infectious Glomerulonephritis (PSGN); low serum C3 is expected during acute presentation and typically normalizes within 6 to 8 weeks.",
        "C. Patient A has Post-Infectious Glomerulonephritis; synpharyngitic onset with normal C3 is the classical hallmark.",
        "D. Patient A has IgA Nephropathy; serum C3 typically drops significantly 2 to 6 weeks after pharyngeal resolution."
